/** @page tarformatsguide TAR Formats

    @tableofcontents

    The module supports multiple TAR formats:

    |!Constant|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_USTAR|POSIX.1-1988 ustar format (100-char filename limit)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_PAX|POSIX.1-2001 pax format (recommended, no practical limits)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_GNU|GNU tar format (extended attributes support)
    |@ref Qore::Tar::TAR_FORMAT_V7|Original V7 Unix tar format (legacy)

    The PAX format is recommended for new archives as it supports:
    - Unlimited filename length
    - Unlimited file size
    - Unicode filenames
    - Extended attributes
    - Nanosecond timestamps

    @section tarformatsnames Entry Names

    Entry names and link targets are UTF-8 strings: names passed to the module in another encoding are
    converted to UTF-8, and names read from an archive are returned as UTF-8 strings.  This does not depend on
    the process locale: the module runs libarchive under a UTF-8 character type locale on the calling thread
    for the duration of each call, so non-ASCII names such as \c 日本語.txt are written, read, looked up and
    extracted exactly even in the \c "C" locale.  The process locale is never changed, and callbacks, such as
    an extraction progress callback, run under the thread's own locale.  A host without any UTF-8 locale
    keeps the locale-dependent behavior.
*/
